      \field{abstract}{Objective Synthesize existing evidence on drones for the distribution of medical supplies. Methodology Scoping review conducted according to Joanna Briggs Institute guidelines and reported in accordance with the PRISMA extension for scoping reviews. A systematic search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Bireme, EBSCO, and TRID through January 20, 2025, to identify case studies worldwide that use drones to distribute medical supplies. Record selection, performed in Rayyan, included a two-stage review of titles and abstracts and full texts, carried out by two reviewers in a blinded and independent manner. Data extraction followed the same approach, and results were synthesized descriptively. Results A total of 452 records were identified; 21 case studies (2018–2025) were finally selected, with experiences on five continents. Drones reduced the average transport time to 45\,min (range 10–120) compared to 305\,min with conventional methods (60\,min–5\,days), equivalent to an absolute average reduction of 260\,min (4.3\,h) and a relative average reduction of 72\% (35–95\% depending on context); In extreme cases, Malawi and Vanuatu, journeys of several days were reduced to {$<$}2\,h. Of 16 records with costs, the median was \$16.07 (2025 USD), the mean was \$93.29 (range 1.16–613.41), with a strongly biased distribution; most were ≤\$20, while a few reflected aggregate costs/CapEx. Conclusion Drones substantially reduce delivery times (median 45\,min vs. 305\,min) and show mainly low unit costs (median \$16.07), but high heterogeneity and the presence of capital costs require standardizing reports and evaluating cost-effectiveness according to scale.}
